    "lawCode": "Amd §1229-c, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3623\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to requiring\nchildren under the age of 8 to be restrained by a child restraint\nsystem in a motor vehicle\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis bill would enhance highway safety for children who are passengers\nin motor vehicles.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Vehicle and Traffic Law (\"VTL\") §§\n1229-c(1)(b) and (c) to raise from seven years to eight years the\nmaximum age at which children over the age of four are required to be\nrestrained in booster seats while they are passengers in the back seat\nof an automobile. This section would also require that all other\npassengers in the back seat, regardless of age, be restrained by a\nsafety belt.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend and renumber VTL § 1229-c(3-a) to\nclarify that a passenger must wear both a lap belt, and a shoulder\nbelt across the torso, when sitting in a vehicle.\n\nSections 3 and 4 of the bill would make conforming amendments to VTL\n§§ 1229-c(6) and (12), respectively, concerning the increased maximum\nage of rear seat child passengers set forth in section 1 of the bill.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would provide that the bill would take effect 90\ndays after it becomes law.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nCurrently, the VTL requires only children between the ages of four and\nseven to be restrained in child passenger restraint systems. There is\nno specific requirement that a shoulder harness belt be worn across\nthe torso.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nA.11083-A, which passed the Assembly in 2007, contained the provisions\nset forth in bill sections 1, 3, and 4 of this bill.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nPursuant to two recent enactments (Chapter 509 of the Laws of 2004 and\nChapter 18 of the Laws of 2005), children between the ages of four and\nseven are required to be restrained in special booster seats, as are\nthose under four who weigh more than forty pounds. However, according\nto the Center for Disease Control growth charts, the average\neight-year-old child weighs about 68 pounds, while 95% of children\nunder age eight weigh less than 80 pounds. All current booster seats\nare certified for at least 80 pounds and many, for up to 100 pounds.\nThe average eight year old is about 50\" tall (4 feet 2 inches) and 96%\nof this age group are under 54\" (4 feet 6 inches).  Due to the\ndifference in anatomy between adults and children, the recommended\nheight to use an adult seat belt is 4 feet 9 inches.  Therefore,\nincreasing the age for required seating in an appropriate child\nrestraint system (\"booster seat\") recognizes the reality of the\nphysical size of this age group, and will enhance the safety of these\nchildren when they are passengers in an automobile.\n\n\nThe bill's provision concerning the proper placement of the shoulder\nharness safety belt is in response to a court decision dismissing a\nticket for a violation of VTL § 1229-c where the defendant was wearing\nthe shoulder harness under her arm. The court held that this was not a\nviolation of the law because VTL § 1229-c (3-a) does not explicitly\nrequire that the belt be worn over the shoulder, but only that the\nwearer be \"restrained\". (People v. Locke, 1 Misc. 3d 482 (Watertown\nCity Court 2003)). Clearly, the intent of existing law is that a\nshoulder harness safety belt be worn across the torso, in order that\nthe belt may be effective. This bill would explicitly delineate that\nintention, so as to properly and effectively restrain and protect\nmotor vehicle occupants.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect 90 days after becoming law.\n",
